# WIN-COMPILER PLAN — Cross-compile + chs_rt port + oodac native on Windows

File: openOODA/audit/win_compiler_plan.oot
Date: 2026-08-19. Owner: openOODA owner. Goal: close ROADMAP.oot line 83.
Scope: Full native lane. From this Linux x86_64 host, `oodac --target windows` produces a Windows .exe; `oodac` and the `ooda` CLI both run natively on Windows; `chs_rt` is ported; a Windows installer is shipped.

## Phase 0 status

Phase 0 is **complete**. After the 4 initial research reports, the orchestrator
ran a real build test on this host and dispatched 7 more sub-agents (K through
Q) for targeted re-walks. Total: 16 files in `openOODA/audit/win_*.oot` plus
1 NSIS skeleton. All under the 256-line cap.

Initial 4:
- `audit/win_toolchain_survey.oot` — 125 lines. Recommends mingw-w64 MSVCRT.
- `audit/win_chs_rt_posix_audit.oot` — 154 lines. 95 POSIX sites, 86 with direct Windows equivalents, 9 need redesign (Landlock, RLIMIT_NOFILE, /dev/null, execlp, /proc/self/cmdline, /dev/tty, clearenv, epoll/inotify/prctl residuals).
- `audit/win_oodac_feasibility.oot` — 175 lines. Compiler source is pure .oo and portable. Three shell invocations in 4 files need a `process_target` table. Build script needs a mingw driver.
- `audit/win_installer_options.oot` — 134 lines. Recommends NSIS for v1.

Sub-agents ran read-only via the `explore` role (one worker for the NSIS
skeleton). No compiler, runtime, cli, or install code was touched.

## 0. Why this is a real lane

ROADMAP.oot line 83 already names it: `[ ] No Windows compiler, CLI, or runtime. Not a second `std`.`
guide/Use.oot and guide/Limits.oot disclaim Mac, Windows, Linux ARM as "not this install." The lane is open, not invented. The std tree is shared (per the ROADMAP row "Not a second std"); only the compiler, CLI, and C runtime are Windows-specific.

